---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE, SITE_URL, PUBLISHER, DISCLAIMER, CATEGORIES } from '../consts';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'AboutPage',
  url: `${SITE_URL}/about`,
  mainEntity: { '@id': `${SITE_URL}/#organization` },
};
---

<Base
  title="About & Methodology"
  description={`Who publishes ${SITE.name}, where the data comes from, and how each figure is verified.`}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article">
      <div class="article__head">
        <p class="kicker">About</p>
        <h1>Who we are, and how we handle numbers</h1>
        <p class="article__dek">{SITE.description}</p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>The publication</h2>
        <p>
          {SITE.name} covers Korean equities, commodities and macroeconomics for readers outside
          Korea. Korean market data is abundant but almost entirely published in Korean, on a
          delay, and in formats built for domestic regulatory use rather than for reading. We take
          the official releases, translate the substance rather than just the words, and say
          plainly what each figure does and does not support.
        </p>
        <p>
          We publish {CATEGORIES.map((c) => c.label).join(', ')} coverage. We do not publish price
          targets, trade ideas, or sponsored content.
        </p>

        <h2 id="sources">Where the data comes from</h2>
        <p>
          Every figure we publish originates from a government or central-bank open-data release
          that permits redistribution. In practice that means:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Equity, index and securitized-product prices</strong> — Financial Services
            Commission open APIs distributed through the
            <a href="https://www.data.go.kr" rel="external noopener" target="_blank"
              >Korea Public Data Portal</a
            >, published under an unrestricted use licence.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Macroeconomic series</strong> — Bank of Korea statistics, accessed through the
            Public Data Portal and the Economic Statistics System.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Listing and issuance reference data</strong> — Financial Services Commission
            listed-securities datasets.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          We do not scrape the Korea Exchange website, and we do not redistribute licensed vendor
          feeds. {SITE.name} is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or licensed by the Korea Exchange.
        </p>

        <h2>Why our numbers are not live</h2>
        <p>
          Korean government open-data feeds settle on a one-business-day lag: a session's figures
          become available from about 1 p.m. the following business day, and Friday's data arrives
          the next Monday. We could not publish intraday prices from these sources even if we
          wanted to, so we do not pretend otherwise.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every article carries an explicit <em>data as-of</em> timestamp in both Korean and US
          Eastern time, on the article card and again in the article itself. If a figure you read
          here is a day behind the live market, that is by design and the timestamp will say so.
        </p>

        <h2>How each article is verified</h2>
        <p>
          Articles are drafted with AI assistance from structured data pulls, then reviewed by a
          human editor before publication. The editor checks three things: that every quoted number
          traces back to a named source and dataset, that the as-of timestamp matches the actual
          release, and that any figure which could not be confirmed has been removed rather than
          softened.
        </p>
        <p>
          The <strong>Data &amp; Verification Notes</strong> block at the foot of every article is
          generated from the article's own source metadata. It lists the exact agency and API used,
          what was cross-checked, and — importantly — what we <em>excluded</em> because it could
          not be verified. If that block is missing, the article does not build.
        </p>

        <h2>Corrections</h2>
        <p>
          If a figure here is wrong, we want to know. Write to
          <a href={`mailto:${PUBLISHER.email}`}>{PUBLISHER.email}</a>. Substantive corrections are
          made in place with a visible updated date; we do not quietly rewrite published numbers.
        </p>

        <h2>Advertising and independence</h2>
        <p>
          {SITE.name} is free to read and funded by display advertising. Advertisers have no
          involvement in, or advance sight of, editorial decisions. We do not accept paid
          placements, sponsored articles, or coverage-for-payment arrangements of any kind.
        </p>

        <h2>Not investment advice</h2>
        <p>{DISCLAIMER.long}</p>

        <h2>Publisher</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>{PUBLISHER.legalName}</strong> ({PUBLISHER.legalNameKo})<br />
          {PUBLISHER.address}<br />
          Business registration number {PUBLISHER.bizRegNo}<br />
          <a href={`mailto:${PUBLISHER.email}`}>{PUBLISHER.email}</a>
        </p>
      </div>

      <a class="backlink" href="/">&larr; Back to the front page</a>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
